When one consumer pins a template’s all-covering result and another matches only
its partial result, the template is instantiated twice (once fully pinned, once
with the unmatched parameter lifted) rather than demoting the partial demand to
a caller-supplied input. UsePair pins F[int, string]; UseKey(Key[bool]) is served
by a second instantiation F[bool, U] with U lifted, not by demoting Key[bool] to
an injector input. (Without demand-aware dispatch, Key[bool] would be silently
turned into a caller input even though F[bool, U] could build it.)
-- config --
import-path: example.com/app
package-name: app
-- example.com/app/a.go --
package app

type Key[T any] struct{}
type Pair[T, U any] struct{}
type A struct{}
type B struct{}

//plumb:build
func F[T, U any]() (Key[T], Pair[T, U]) { return Key[T]{}, Pair[T, U]{} }

//plumb:build
func UsePair(p Pair[int, string]) *A { return &A{} }

//plumb:build
func UseKey(k Key[bool]) *B { return &B{} }
